## Key decisions and recommended posture

| Decision | Recommended posture |
|---|---|
| Management unit | Directional country corridor, customer type, and payout method |
| Licensing | Own licenses in strategic scale markets; partners elsewhere |
| Compliance | Common group core with jurisdiction-specific overlays |
| Settlement | Central oversight with locally compliant accounts and liquidity |
| Prefunding | Dynamic, capped, and charged to corridor economics |
| FX | Hedge transactional exposure; avoid speculative positions |
| Capital controls | Comply, restrict, reprice, or suspend—never circumvent |
| Partners | Tier by control and technical maturity; require redundancy for critical routes |
| Pricing | Fully loaded and risk-adjusted |
| Technology | Standard internal APIs around heterogeneous local partners |
| Expansion | Pilot and scale through formal gates |
| Exit | Use predetermined legal, risk, liquidity, and economic triggers |
| Governance | Central standards with accountable local licensed entities |

## Conclusion

The sustainable model is a **federated corridor network**: common technology, compliance intelligence, treasury discipline, and profitability standards combined with locally valid licenses, settlement arrangements, controls, and banking partnerships.

The firm should:

1. Reconstruct the portfolio as discrete directional corridors;
2. Establish a defensible license and funds-flow model for each;
3. Apply common compliance capabilities with strict local overlays;
4. Control prefunding, partner credit, FX, and trapped liquidity centrally;
5. Price every corridor for its full compliance and settlement burden;
6. Build redundancy around strategically important local partners;
7. respond to capital controls through documented restrictions or suspension rather than routing workarounds;
8. Scale only after legal, operational, reconciliation, and economic gates are met.

This approach may reduce headline corridor count and eliminate some superficially attractive volume. It will, however, produce a more resilient network with better regulatory credibility, lower principal risk, clearer partner economics, and stronger capacity to expand when market conditions permit.
